{
  "name": "421. BFL image tasks and the four image task routes (bfl image tasks)",
  "description": "Pins TXE-6431 (contract C2, C4 to C7, C9; work item BF16; BFL vendor sheet B2 to B10) on the image task routes. Local fixture regression, no live provider calls: start tests/e2e/api/runners/provider-error-fixture.mjs, start an isolated gateway on tests/e2e/api/provider_config/provider-error-fixture.config.json and run with providerErrorFixture=1; every case is skipped otherwise. The config points the bfl provider at the fixture's /pp/bfl, which answers BFL's AsyncResponse to a submit and BFL's ResultResponse to GET /v1/get_result by task id. Row 2 creates a generation task on flux-pro-1.1 and reads the task object: queued, an id {vendor id}:bfl, the credit cost and megapixels; row 3 reads the bytes BFL received: size as width and height, callback_url as webhook_url, an extra field merged unchanged, no unified name left. Row 4 queries the task: completed, the sample link in data[], expires_at ten minutes after BFL's end_time, the settled cost in credits. Row 5 queries a moderated task: failed, the vendor's code, content_filter with BFL's reasons. Rows 6 to 9 are refused with caller_unsupported_parameter before any upstream call: n 2, client_reference_id (BFL has no external id), callback_url on flux-3-image (no webhook_url), a mask on a model other than fill. Row 10 is the cancel route: 400 provider_cancel_unsupported, BFL has no cancel API. Row 11 reads the fixture: exactly one submit arrived and each query once, so no refused request reached BFL. Before the change none of the four routes existed. Per-model conversion, the nine statuses one to one, the id's polling host and the FLUX 3 link lifetime are pinned by core/providers/bfl's Go tests.",
  "item": [
    {
